spec:
Write `parse_ini(text: str) -> dict[str, dict[str, str]]` for an INI-like format. Process `text.splitlines()`; strip whitespace from each line, then:
- blank lines are skipped;
- lines starting with `;` or `#` are comments and skipped;
- a line starting with `[` must end with `]` and contain a non-empty name (after stripping the name inside); it starts that section (the name is stripped). Reopening a section name continues it. Otherwise (no closing `]` or empty name) raise `ValueError`;
- any other line is `key = value`, split at the FIRST `=`; key and value are both stripped. A value may be empty (`""`) or contain further `=` characters, and no inline comments exist (a `;` inside a value is part of the value). A missing `=` or an empty key raises `ValueError`. A repeated key in a section overwrites the earlier value.
Keys that appear before any section header belong to the section named `""` (empty string); that section appears in the result only if it has at least one key. Every named section appears in the result even if it has no keys. All values are strings.

Example: `parse_ini("[a]\nx = 1\n; note\ny=2=3")` -> `{"a": {"x": "1", "y": "2=3"}}`

